      "writtenOn": "2018-09-13T08:44:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The followings are descriptions of a bit-wise IEEE floating-point standard single precision (32-bit) NaN:\ns111 1111 1xxx xxxx xxxx xxxx xxxx xxxx where s is the sign (most often ignored in applications) and the x sequence represents a non-zero number (the value zero encodes infinities). The first bit from x is used to determine the type of NaN: \"quiet NaN\" or \"signaling NaN\". The remaining bits encode a payload (most often ignored in applications).\n\nSo I think it should be OK no matter we use 0xffc00000 or 0xffffffff as the quiet NaN. Do you want me to change it to 0xffffffff?",
      "parentUuid": "2690ac9b_51acedea",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 114,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 114,
        "endChar": 22
      },
      "revId": "81b60d2090aecfb875ec79bf775ba3334bc3353f",
